{
  "round": 14,
  "pair": {
    "A": "the v2.2.0 build on Electron 31",
    "B": "the same v2.2.0 tree on Electron 44 (the v2.3 runtime)"
  },
  "note": "Round 14 is the v2.3 release gate: the same v2.2.0 tree under two runtimes, Electron 31 (arm A) and Electron 44 (arm B). Every task is expected to tie by construction — the application code is byte-identical across the arms — so any result is a statement about the runtime, or noise.",
  "corrections": [
    {
      "task": "VC3",
      "columns": [
        "task"
      ],
      "from": "A",
      "to": "tie",
      "rule": "a difference that would vanish under identical compositor ticks is not a difference",
      "why": "Awarded to the Electron 31 arm because three tool-call headers, the selected conversation title and the Rollback/Export actions in the Electron 44 arm’s dark capture were drawn in the LIGHT theme’s ink — 1.05:1, dark on dark — 700ms after the switch; the critic gave it low confidence and asked whether identical tokens would remove it. Settled by experiment rather than argument. (1) Re-run VC3 twice on each arm through the same driver: 0 of 4 captures show any light-ink node, so across the round it is 1 of 3 on Electron 44 and 0 of 3 on Electron 31. (2) A direct probe that boots the built app on each runtime, flips the theme class exactly as App.tsx does and polls computed colours: on BOTH Electron 31 and 44 every element carrying `transition-colors` held the light ink for more than three seconds while plain text switched within 100ms, and setting `transition: none` on such an element switched it at once. The elements are sitting in a 150ms colour transition that only advances when the compositor ticks, which a covered window does not get. Which arm’s window was covered at the moment of capture is a fact about the desktop, not the runtime. The weakness is the app’s and is real: a reader who switches theme while the window is throttled sees unreadable text for as long as it stays throttled. Fixed in v2.3 by suspending transitions for the two frames a theme switch takes (App.tsx, index.css), pinned by a new style check that measures the guarded switch on a transition-colors element in a real window and reports what an unguarded one reads."
    }
  ],
  "caveats": [
    "Blinding was partial on sight: with the launcher shim as the entry, the sidebar version badge fell back to Electron's own version, so every screenshot footer read v31.7.7 or v44.1.1. Every critic reported the strings and set them aside; the arms were not named by them, but a critic could tell which runtime a run was on. The capture now writes a package.json beside the shim so the badge shows the arm's app version.",
    "The harness gained two fixes before this round could run at all: the per-run profile moved out of the repository (macOS folder privacy refused the re-signed Electron renames under ~/Documents) and the driver probes for the WebSocket flag Node 22+ rejects. Both arms were captured by the same, fixed harness.",
    "All 36 runs were VALID on the first pass; no re-runs."
  ],
